# Python
.venv/
.venv-tts/
__pycache__/
*.pyc

# Rendered video and intermediate frames — regenerable in ~10 min per video,
# and too large to belong in git history.
renders/*.mp4
# ...and one level deeper. `renders/*.mp4` does not match
# `renders/superseded-*/foo.mp4`, so moving four superseded renders into a
# subdirectory on 2026-09-05 staged 183 MB of video that the pattern above
# exists to keep out. The READMEs in those directories ARE committed - the
# record of what was superseded and why is the part worth keeping.
renders/**/*.mp4
renders/*.work/
renders/*.png
visuals/sample/
visuals/_ext_test/
*.log

# Voice reference audio. Deliberately excluded: this is the owner's own voice,
# and voice biometrics should not live in a repository that could ever be shared
# or made public. BACK THIS UP SEPARATELY — it is not reproducible.
voice/*.wav
voice/*.m4a

# Large source archive kept locally
source/*.zip

# Local secrets — never commit.
# The whole directory is ignored, not a list of filenames: a deny-list only
# covers the names someone thought of, and `client_secret.json` matched none of
# the four patterns that used to live here. Anything credential-shaped goes in
# .secrets/ and nothing in .secrets/ is ever committable.
.secrets/
.env
*.key
*.pem
credentials*.json
client_secret*.json
*token*.json
*_api_key*
service-account*.json

# Loop: visual plans are regenerated from the frozen work copy in loop/work/,
# the same way renders/ are. The work copies themselves ARE committed — they are
# the record of exactly what was spoken in a given week.
loop/plans/

# Generated narration. Same class as renders/*.mp4 above: large binary output that
# the pipeline reproduces from scripts/ and voice/ on demand. Added 2026-08-31 after
# `git add audio/` staged 176 wavs - renders were ignored and audio was not, so the
# rule only covered half the generated media. The beats.json manifests ARE tracked;
# they are small and they are the audio-to-plan contract.
audio/**/*.wav

# Vertical Shorts cut from renders/*-final.mp4 by bin/make-shorts.sh. Same class
# as renders/*.mp4 above: regenerable output, too large for git history.
shorts/

# wrangler's own logs and state, created in the repo root by any `wrangler r2`
# command loop/r2.py runs. Never useful in history, and it grows a file per run.
.wrangler/

# The Mac's copies of loop-state files that upstream overwrote (loop/mac_sync.py)
loop/state/_local/

# Comment watch: the ledger is tracked (it is the record of who instructed
# what); the per-run digest and act receipt are transient.
loop/state/comments/digest.json
loop/state/comments/last_act.json

# The video rights manifest, per loop/footage_lane.py's own docstring: "the
# manifest itself never enters git — the clips are 5 GB on the Mac and in R2,
# and V11 re-hashes every record against the bytes on disk, so a committed
# manifest with no clips beside it would fail the Monday lane." What crosses
# to other hosts is loop/state/harvest_runs.json (the stamp), which IS
# tracked. The 2026-09-21 Mac-state handoff commit (b1cd518) committed this
# file by accident under a blanket "commit everything so the checkout is
# clean" — 8,453 lines with no clips beside them — and broke
# test_footage_harvest_is_honest.py's "video_rights.json has never existed"
# assumption on every host, including this one. Untracked here; the working
# copy on the Mac is unaffected.
channel/imagery/video_rights.json
